%% The Topic 2 key diagrams, redrawn simply — RN volume only (included by RN.gsm).

## The key diagrams

Four diagrams carry most of Topic 2. Learn to draw each one from memory in **under two minutes**. A diagram placed early in an answer shows the examiner the whole argument at a glance and earns credit in its own right.

::: fig spectrum | The schools of feminism — from reform to transformation.
@left Reform the system
@right Transform the system
10: Liberal — laws
30: Men's feminism — masculinity
45: Psychoanalytic — childrearing
60: Postmodern — language
78: Marxist and socialist — economy
94: Radical — patriarchy itself
:::

**Draw it in:** "any two schools" (2022) and "which feminism suits Pakistan?" (2018). It shows that you see the whole field.

::: fig compare | Radical and socialist feminism.
## Radical
- Root: patriarchy
- Women as a "sex class"
- Site: body, sexuality, family
- Millett, Firestone, Hanisch
## Socialist
- Root: capitalism + patriarchy
- Women divided by class too
- Site: paid work and housework
- Mitchell, Hartmann, Eisenstein
:::

**Draw it in:** radical versus socialist feminism (2016 — exactly this comparison), radical feminism (2024 note) and Marxist feminism (2021).

::: fig flow | Chodorow — how mothering reproduces inequality.
@per 3
Women do early care
Girls stay close: a caring self
Boys separate: "not-mother"
Boys devalue the feminine
Girls grow up to mother
Remedy: shared parenting
:::

**Draw it in:** psychoanalytic feminism (2021).

::: fig venn | Intersectionality — overlapping systems, one position.
@centre Poor, rural, minority woman
Gender: patriarchal norms
Class and place: poor, rural
Religion or ethnicity: a minority
:::

**Draw it in:** intersectionality (2025), and Topic 5's 2026 question. Name **Crenshaw (1989)** and **Collins (1990)** beside it.
